\begin_layout Title
Does Playing Football Affect Future Outcomes?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
November 7, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Over the last three decades, the popularity of football, especially pro
 football, has gradually increased.
 A little more then one-third of U.S.
 adults who say they follow at least one sport identify football as their
 favorite.
 As one can imagine, this rise in popularity has transformed the NFL into
 an approximately billion dollar industry.
 To elaborate, the operating income of the league's 32 squared was $984.5
 million in 2005, and the average NFL team is worth $898 million.
 However, the football's large following has only strengthened the scrunity
 it endures concerning player safety.
 In recent years, reports about the NFL's abysmal concussion policy have
 began to permeate through the national discussion.
 In fact, many ex-players have sued the NFL over their lax rules and policies
 on concussions and player safety.
 Moreover, researcers are just now discovering the significant negative
 correlaton between head trauma (i.e., concussions) and brain health.
 Each concussion is a serious brain injury that could have lasting negative
 effects.
 Doctors believe that even several mild concussions that happen within a
 short time of one another can cause long-term disabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One of the more pressing arguments lobbied against football is the potential
 negative effects it can have on the long-term development and health of
 younger players.
 The effects of head trauma in youth sports is an area of research that
 has been largely overlooked.
 Yet, current studies have produced some truly startling results.
 According to a recent article published in the New York Times, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

High school football players are nearly twice as likely to get concussions
 as college football players and high school atheles in other sports, and
 as many as 20 percent of athlets who get a concussion have symptoms that
 linger more than two weeks, according to the Institute of Medicine
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (Belson, 2013, para.
 1).
 Within economics, there is a growing area of research that studies the
 role early life experiences have in determining future life outcomes.
 Based on the previously mentioned findings, one can infer that participation
 in youth football programs could signficantly impact human captial accumulation
 during the formative years of development, growth, and maturity.
 Tackling this issue provides an excellent setup to perform truly noteworthy
 research.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, I wish to answer the following question: does participating in youth
 football programs affect future outcomes? To answer this question, I propose
 a differences-in-differences method with no time element, similar to the
 one presented by Nunn and Puga (2010).
 In my setup, the treatment variable will not be binary, but it can instead
 realize integer values.
 The treatment group consists of individuals who played organized football
 anytime prior to college.
 Note that we will only focus on subjects who elected not to play organized
 football beyond high school because it can be argued that individuals who
 play football in college and beyond are afforded more opportunities for
 succes than those who did not.
 The control group are individuals who did not play any kind of organized
 football while growing up.
 During my estimation, I would control for region fixed effects, socio-economic
 background, race, education, parent's eductation, and physical characteristics.
 Thus, after controlling for all of these pertinent factors, my identifying
 assumption would be that absent treatment, the future outcomes of individuals
 in the treatment group would have closely followed the same general pattern
 of future outcomes as those in the control group.
\end_layout

